1	/**
2	 * The coverage report: what the fleet pipeline actually proved, and on what.
3	 *
4	 * This document exists because `versionless run` ends by pointing at a coverage
5	 * number, and a coverage number is exactly the kind of claim that inflates
6	 * quietly. Three properties keep it from doing so, and each is a mechanism
7	 * rather than an intention.
8	 *
9	 * **One derivation, not two.** Every counted cell, holdout, demotion, boundary
10	 * and capability figure here is read off the same support matrix the enterprise
11	 * report and `supported-matrix` already publish — `buildSupportMatrix`, off the
12	 * Judge counting ledger. Nothing is recounted locally, so this surface cannot
13	 * disagree with that one; widening what reads the derivation does not widen the
14	 * claim it carries.
15	 *
16	 * **The intervention rule.** An application whose status derives from a
17	 * `versionless run` record is recorded proven only when that record carries an
18	 * intervention count of zero. A record that does not assert an intervention
19	 * count cannot produce a proven application at all: it is recorded
20	 * `not-admitted` with `intervention-count-not-asserted`, because "nobody
21	 * measured the hand-help" and "there was none" are not the same statement. The
22	 * rule reaches run-record-derived rows only — the sealed cells the Judge already
23	 * counted are not re-adjudicated by a counter that did not exist when they were
24	 * proven.
25	 *
26	 * **The same honesty guard.** The human rendering terminates in
27	 * `assertEnterpriseSurfaceHonesty`, so a bounded outcome restated as a generic
28	 * pass, a dropped boundary prevalence figure or blanket-support vocabulary stops
29	 * the render here rather than reaching a reader.
30	 *
31	 * Both files are re-derived from the canonical receipts and compared at
32	 * verification time — the JSON canonically, the Markdown byte for byte — so an
33	 * edit to either fails verification rather than changing a claim.
34	 */
